diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket claims that the IT department (192.168.1.x) is completely cut off from the CS department (192.168.2.x) and that router0 has "dropped its CS side." Direct probing of the live lab finds no evidence of any fault. Router0's eth1 interface (the CS-facing link at 192.168.2.1/24) is physically UP and carrier-present. Its eth0 (IT-facing, 192.168.1.1/24) and eth2 (WAN, 10.10.0.1/8) are equally healthy. All five CS hosts — cs_hod (192.168.2.2), cs_lab1 (192.168.2.3), cs_lab2 (192.168.2.4), cs_lab3 (192.168.2.5), and cs_lab4 (192.168.2.6) — respond to pings from it_hod with zero packet loss and sub-2 ms RTT. Connectivity in the reverse direction (cs_hod → it_hod) is equally clean. Router0's RIP configuration advertises both the IT and CS subnets correctly, and its routing table shows 192.168.2.0/24 as a directly connected route via eth1. Switch1 (the L2 bridge between router0 and the CS LAN) has both ports UP and properly bridged. There are no iptables or nftables rules blocking forwarded traffic. In summary, the symptom described in the ticket does not exist in the current network state — this is a false-premise report. No corrective action is required; the IT-to-CS path through router0 is fully operational.
=== END ===